So the web client ALSO seeks if `target` (MEDIA_POSITION) differs from `lastReported` by >0.25s. Same logic as GTK's should_seek! So if MEDIA_POSITION stays 0 and lastReported advances, the web client would ALSO seek to 0...

But the user only reports GTK. Why doesn't the web skip back? Let me think:
- On the web, MEDIA_POSITION = seekRequest = 0 (unchanged). As the audio plays, lastReported advances (1, 2, 3...). A MEDIA_POSITION delta of 0 arrives only ONCE (at mount). After that, no more MEDIA_POSITION deltas (seekRequest unchanged). So the web client's `applyProperty(PROP_MEDIA_POSITION)` is NOT called again — the seek check only runs when a MEDIA_POSITION DELTA arrives! Since seekRequest doesn't change, no further checks → no seek. 

So on the WEB, the seek only happens when MEDIA_POSITION is re-emitted (a change). seekRequest=0 → emitted once → no repeat seeks. The audio plays fine.

But in the GTK renderer, the reconcile RE-READS MEDIA_POSITION from the node's properties on EVERY reconcile (every frame/delta), regardless of whether the property changed. So `should_seek(last_reported, target=0)` runs repeatedly as playback advances → seeks back to 0. THAT'S THE DIVERGENCE!

The web client applies MEDIA_POSITION only on a delta (change); GTK applies it on every reconcile (re-reading the property).

So the fix options:
1. **GTK: only seek when MEDIA_POSITION actually CHANGED** (track the last-applied seek target; only run should_seek when the property value differs from what we last applied). This mirrors the web client's delta-only behavior.
2. GTK: treat MEDIA_POSITION == the last_reported (echo) as no-op (already done via should_seek), but ALSO handle the "unchanged target" case — track the last MEDIA_POSITION value applied; if unchanged, skip the seek check.

The cleanest fix mirroring the web: in the GTK renderer, only evaluate the seek when the MEDIA_POSITION property value has changed since the last time we applied it. I.e., store `last_seek_target` in MediaPlayer; if the new target equals the last-applied target (or within epsilon), don't re-check `should_seek` (or don't seek). Actually — the `should_seek` guard already handles the echo. The problem is the unchanging target (0) vs advancing last_reported.

Hmm — but wait: what if the user drags the seek slider to 30s, plays, and the position advances to 35s while MEDIA_POSITION stays 30? The web client: after the initial seek to 30, no more MEDIA_POSITION deltas → no re-seek → plays from 30. GTK: on every reconcile, should_seek(last_reported=35, target=30) → |35-30| > 0.25 → SEEK BACK to 30! Same bug — after any seek, playback gets yanked back to the seek target as it advances.

So the GTK bug: it re-applies the seek every reconcile because it re-reads the (unchanged) MEDIA_POSITION. The fix: **only seek when MEDIA_POSITION changes** — mirror the web's delta semantics. Track the last-applied target; skip the seek check if the target is unchanged.

