```java
final class SeekControl implements WritableSignal<Float> {
    private final WritableSignal<Float> position;
    private final WritableSignal<Float> seekRequest;
    private boolean editing;
    private float pendingSeek;

    SeekControl(...) {...}

    @Override
    public Float get() {
        return position.get();
    }

    @Override
    public void set(Float seconds) {
        pendingSeek = seconds;
        // During a drag the thumb is authoritative; only the display's position
        // updates (harmless; the renderers suppress inbound VALUE in-flight). The
        // seek COMMAND is committed on release (onEditingChanged(false)) so the
        // player seeks once per drag, not on every tick.
        if (!editing) {
            commit();
        }
    }

    /** EDITING_CHANGED boundary: a drag session began (true) / ended (false). */
    public void onEditingChanged(boolean editing) {
        this.editing = editing;
        if (!editing) {
            commit();
        }
    }

    private void commit() {
        position.set(pendingSeek);
        seekRequest.set(pendingSeek);
    }

    @Override
    public void update(UnaryOperator<Float> fn) {
        set(fn.apply(get()));
    }

    @Override
    public Signal<Float> asReadonly() {
        return this::get;
    }
}
```

Wait — should `set` update `position` during editing? The in-flight suppression means the renderer ignores inbound VALUE for the slider while dragging. But the `position` signal is ALSO read by the subtitle computed and the seek slider display. During editing, updating position would re-emit the slider's VALUE (suppressed) and update the subtitle. Hmm — do we want the subtitle to change during a drag? It's a minor thing. To keep it clean, during editing I'll NOT touch position either (just buffer pendingSeek). On release, commit sets position + seekRequest → the seek fires, MEDIA_TIME_UPDATED resumes, position updates naturally. So during the drag, position stays at the pre-drag value (the renderer's in-flight suppression keeps the thumb at the drag position). Good — simpler.

