- `FALLBACK_SVG` literal (the circle inner markup).
- `pathlandBase()`: from `document.documentElement.dataset.pathlandBase ?? "/_pathland"`.
- `setIcon(svg: SVGElement | Element, name: string): void`:
  - If name is empty/null → set FALLBACK_SVG? Current behavior: `ICONS[text] ?? FALLBACK_SVG` — a missing ICON_NAME value (undefined) would fall back. Keep: empty/undefined name → fallback.
  - Resolve synchronously: `cache.get(name)` else `glyphInDom(name)` else FALLBACK.
  - Set `svg.innerHTML = resolved`.
  - If the resolve came from a real embedded glyph (cache or DOM) → done.
  - If it didn't (fell back) AND never requested → kick the fetch: `fetchAndCache(name).then(() => { if still valid, svg.innerHTML = cache.get(name) })`. Only repatch this specific svg.
- We need the fetch to run once per name even when multiple svgs request concurrently. Use `inflight: Map<string, Promise<void>>`. And `requested` set so a later miss doesn't refire a completed-but-failed fetch repeatedly — actually memoize failures too: store fallback in cache? If fetch fails, cache the FALLBACK so we don't refetch on every later toggle. But network could be transient... Better: cache failures with the fallback so a failed send doesn't spam; a page reload refetches. I'll cache the resolved value (whether glyph or fallback) in `cache`.
- `glyphInDom(name)`: `document.querySelector(CSS.escape?)` — attr selector with a CSS-escaped name: `` `svg.pathland-icon[data-pathland-icon="${CSS.escape(name)}"]` ``. In happy-dom, is `CSS.escape` available? happy-dom exposes `CSS`? Possibly not. Since tests use happy-dom, and `CSS.escape` may be missing in the test env. Safer: use attribute-value equality without CSS escaping, but that could break on names with special chars — canonical names are plain ASCII (letters, hyphens) so a direct attr selector is fine. To be robust AND test-friendly, avoid CSS.escape: iterate `querySelectorAll('svg.pathland-icon[data-pathland-icon]')` and match `getAttribute` exactly. That's a bit more code but immune to escaping/CSS.escape availability. Given icons are rare per page, a queryAll + find is fine.
- fetch URL: `` `${pathlandBase()}/icons/${encodeURIComponent(name)}.svg` ``.
- Extract inner markup: response text is `WEB_SVG_OPEN + inner + </svg>`. inner = `text.slice(text.indexOf(">") + 1, text.lastIndexOf("</svg>"))`. Robust enough (we control format; WEB_SVG_OPEN's first `>` is after viewBox). Actually WEB_SVG_OPEN = `<svg class="pathland-icon" xmlns="..." viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor">` — first `>` is the open tag's close. Good.
