Sorting: the spec says candidates sorted ascending (App transmits ascending). The renderer should TRUST the ascending order (app responsibility) or sort defensively? Spec says "ascending; equal → lower index wins". With ascending + stable, `max{i: t[i] <= w}` picks the LAST threshold ≤ w, which for equal thresholds picks the highest index among equals — NOT lower! The spec says equal thresholds pick lower index. So the rule must be: among thresholds ≤ w, choose the LOWEST index for ties. To do that with a sorted list and equality: scan from the END, but on ties prefer the first occurrence. Simplest correct: find the largest index i such that t[i] <= w AND (i == 0 || t[i] != t[i-1]... no). Cleaner: iterate and keep the LAST STRICTLY-INCREASING breakpoint ≤ w. i.e., find max i where t[i] <= w, but if t[i] == t[i-1], use i-1. Implementation: 
```
let mut idx = 0; // fallback
for (i, t) in thresholds.iter().enumerate() {
    if *t <= width {
        // prefer the FIRST of a run of equal thresholds
        if i == 0 || thresholds[i-1] != thresholds[i] { idx = i; }
    } else { break; } // sorted — done
}
```
